## What I planned

Task 1 — #838: `/read` and `/plan` mode do not block `git commit` (Kind: product)

Measured yesterday, not inferred: detect_write_command("git commit -m 'x'") and analyze_bash_command(...) both return None. Two independent enumerations of "what writes" — a write-verb table and a destructive-pattern classifier — and neither contains the verb this agent uses most. So in a mode whose entire promise is mechanical enforcement at the tool layer, git commit, git checkout, `git reset --hard` all walk through.

The task's load-bearing details: step 0 is grep the consumers of `detect_write_command` before touching it (fold it in only if ReadModeGuardTool is the sole one); the classifier fails closed on an unrecognised subcommand with the read-only set enumerated explicitly; global options before the subcommand (git -C . commit) get their own table row; and the near-miss guard is non-negotiable — `git status/log/diff` must still pass or the fix trades a silent hole for a silently unusable mode.

